{"builder":"Goal: one pull request that adds a --shout flag to the greet CLI and meets every expect line of https://github.com/o/greet-cli/issues/12:\n1. node bin/greet.js --shout <name> prints the greeting in upper case\n2. without --shout the output is unchanged\n3. docs/index.md documents --shout\n4. after merge, the published docs page at https://o.github.io/greet-cli/ lists --shout\n\nSteps:\n1. Read bin/greet.js, src/greet.js, test/greet.test.js, docs/index.md and .github/workflows/pages.yml. Right now bin/greet.js takes the first argument that does not start with `--` as the name, ignores any other flags, and prints `Hello, <name>!`. The pages workflow publishes the docs/ directory to GitHub Pages on every push to main.\n2. In bin/greet.js, check whether `--shout` is among the arguments. If it is, print the greeting in upper case (for example `node bin/greet.js --shout Ada` prints `HELLO, ADA!` and a newline). The flag must work whether it comes before or after the name. Leave everything else as it is: without --shout, the output, the usage message and the exit status must be exactly what they are now (`node bin/greet.js Ada` still prints `Hello, Ada!` and a newline). Do not change what greeting() in src/greet.js returns. If you add a helper, keep it separate.\n3. In test/greet.test.js, add tests that run the CLI with `--shout Ada` and with `Ada --shout` and expect `HELLO, ADA!\\n`. Keep the existing tests as they are.\n4. In docs/index.md, document the --shout flag: show its usage (for example `node bin/greet.js --shout <name>`) and say that it prints the greeting in upper case. Keep the existing content.\n5. Do not change .github/workflows/pages.yml. It already publishes docs/ after merge, so the --shout text in docs/index.md is what will put --shout on https://o.github.io/greet-cli/. Do not try to deploy or publish anything yourself.\n6. Run `npm test` and make sure it passes. Also run `node bin/greet.js --shout Ada`, `node bin/greet.js Ada --shout` and `node bin/greet.js Ada` by hand and check what they print.\n7. Open one pull request with these changes. Its description should say that the published page will only show --shout after merge, once the pages workflow has run.","verifier":"Check the pull request for https://github.com/o/greet-cli/issues/12 at its head commit. Run every check from the repository root at the head. Run `npm test` once first and note the result. There is one check per expect line, in order.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"node bin/greet.js --shout <name> prints the greeting in upper case\"\nRun: `node bin/greet.js --shout Ada` and `node bin/greet.js --shout 'Grace Hopper'`.\nPASS: each command prints the greeting for that name in upper case (for `Ada` that is `HELLO, ADA!`).\nFAIL: either command prints anything that is not in upper case, prints a greeting that is not for that name, prints nothing, or crashes.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"without --shout the output is unchanged\"\nRun: at the head, run `node bin/greet.js Ada`, `node bin/greet.js 'Grace Hopper'` and `node bin/greet.js` (with no name), saving stdout and stderr. Then run the same commands at the PR's base commit (for example `git worktree add` in a temporary directory, or `git stash`-free checkout of the base in a separate worktree) and compare.\nPASS: for each command, stdout and stderr at the head match the base exactly (for `Ada` that is `Hello, Ada!` and a newline).\nFAIL: the output of any of these commands differs between the head and the base.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"docs/index.md documents --shout\"\nRun: read docs/index.md at the head, for example with `grep -n -- '--shout' docs/index.md` and then read the text around each match.\nPASS: docs/index.md mentions --shout and says what it does (that it makes the greeting upper case / louder).\nFAIL: docs/index.md does not mention --shout, or mentions it without saying what it does.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"after merge, the published docs page at https://o.github.io/greet-cli/ lists --shout\"\nThe live page can only be seen after merge, so do not fetch or read https://o.github.io/greet-cli/. Instead, check what the head contains that will produce the page.\nRun: read .github/workflows/pages.yml at the head and check that it still publishes the docs directory (an upload-pages-artifact step with `path: docs` and a deploy-pages step) on push to the default branch. Check that docs/index.md is inside that directory and contains --shout (see check 3).\nPASS: the head's pages workflow still publishes docs/ on push to the default branch, and docs/index.md in that directory lists --shout. Say in your report that the live published page can only be seen after merge.\nFAIL: the pages workflow was removed, or no longer publishes docs/, or the published directory does not contain a file that lists --shout.\n\nAdvisory (does not decide PASS or FAIL):\n- Exact upper-case output for `node bin/greet.js --shout Ada` is expected to be `HELLO, ADA!` followed by a newline, exit status 0.\n- Try `node bin/greet.js Ada --shout` too; the flag should work after the name.\n- `npm test` should pass, and there should be tests covering --shout.\n- greeting() in src/greet.js should still return `Hello, <name>!`.\n- Note any changes outside bin/greet.js, src/greet.js, test/greet.test.js and docs/index.md."}